 ## Overview
-The Light Cache Rendering Pipeline is a direct volume ray-casting technique that pre-computes lighting distributions inside a volume and stores them in an illumination cache, drastically improving rendering performance [S1, S2, unofficial]. Unlike standard ray-marching which recalculates illumination per frame, this method exploits the assumption of isotropic light scattering to separate volume density evaluation from lighting computation, allowing for fast updates when only light positions change [S1, S2, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from siblings like Radiance Caching by using aggregate queries on a 3D Summed Area Table (SAT) for speed, yielding results similar in quality to dense sampling but much faster [S2, unofficial].
+The Light Cache Rendering Pipeline is a GPU volume ray-casting technique that pre-computes lighting distributions inside a volume and stores them in an illumination cache, drastically improving rendering performance [S1, S2, unofficial]. Unlike standard ray-marching which recalculates illumination per frame, this method exploits the assumption of isotropic light scattering to separate volume density evaluation from lighting computation, allowing for fast updates when only light positions change [S1, S2, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from siblings like Radiance Caching by using aggregate queries on a 3D Summed Area Table (SAT) for speed, yielding results similar in quality to dense sampling but much faster [S2, unofficial].
 
